WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.904 --> 00:00:19.186
wanted to walk on the moor before it got dark, and I agreed to accompany him.

2
00:00:19.206 --> 00:00:23.008
"I think I'll just put this horseshoe in my pocket for luck," he said.

3
00:00:23.809 --> 00:00:29.072
Colonel Ross, who'd shown signs of impatience at Holmes' quiet, systematic methods, gave an irritated sigh.

4
00:00:29.856 --> 00:00:32.078
"Please come back to Straker's house with me, Inspector Gregory.

5
00:00:32.378 --> 00:00:34.300
There are several points on which I'd like your advice."

6
00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.304
The colonel bowed to us, and then he and Gregory turned back toward the house.

7
00:00:39.204 --> 00:00:42.948
The sun was beginning to sink and the sloping plain in front of us was bathed in gold.

8
00:00:43.768 --> 00:00:47.051
But the glories of the landscape were wasted on Holmes, who was deep in thought.

9
00:00:48.012 --> 00:00:52.196
At last he broke his silence and suggested that we focus on Silver Blaze instead of the murder.

10
00:00:52.216 --> 00:00:56.640
"A horse is a very sociable animal, Watson," said Holmes.

11
00:00:57.564 --> 00:01:00.045
"Assuming he broke away during the murder, where would he go?

12
00:01:00.925 --> 00:01:03.607
He'd either return to his own stable or go over to Mapleton.

13
00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:05.907
Why should he run wild upon the moor?"

14
00:01:05.927 --> 00:01:08.969
"Maybe the Gypsies kidnapped him," I suggested.

15
00:01:08.989 --> 00:01:13.250
"No, they always clear out when they hear of trouble because they don't want to be pestered by

16
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:14.791
the police," explained Holmes.

17
00:01:14.811 --> 00:01:18.373
"So it would be too risky for them to try to sell such a horse.

18
00:01:19.253 --> 00:01:21.074
The Gypsies would have gained nothing by taking him."

19
00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:23.094
"Then where is Silver Blaze?"

20
00:01:23.995 --> 00:01:27.441
"He's not at Colonel Ross' stable, so he must be at Mapleton," said Holmes.

21
00:01:27.461 --> 00:01:29.422
"Let's assume that for now.

22
00:01:30.243 --> 00:01:32.065
You can see that the moor dips toward Mapleton.

23
00:01:33.566 --> 00:01:36.529
That long hollow over there must have been very wet on Monday night.

24
00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.732
I'm willing to bet that we'll find some tracks over there."

25
00:01:40.492 --> 00:01:44.376
We had been walking briskly during our conversation, and in a few minutes we reached the hollow.

26
00:01:45.297 --> 00:01:48.760
At Holmes' request I walked down the bank to the right while he went to the left.

27
00:01:49.721 --> 00:01:53.124
I had not walked more than fifty paces before he gave a shout and waved to me.

28
00:01:53.888 --> 00:01:57.309
When I reached him, I plainly saw the tracks of a horse in the mud in front of

29
00:01:57.349 --> 00:02:01.530
him. The horseshoe that he removed from his pocket matched the impression on the ground.

30
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.771
"See the value of imagination?"

31
00:02:04.151 --> 00:02:04.691
said Holmes.

32
00:02:04.711 --> 00:02:07.532
"This is the one quality that Gregory lacks.

33
00:02:08.392 --> 00:02:10.893
We imagined where the horse might go, and we were right."

34
00:02:11.693 --> 00:02:17.134
We continued to walk toward Mapleton, sometimes losing the horse's tracks in the hard, dry dirt, but picking

35
00:02:17.154 --> 00:02:18.255
them up again where it was wet.

36
00:02:19.795 --> 00:02:22.836
When we were quite close to the stable, we found the tracks once again.

37
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.382
Only this time a man's tracks were visible next to the horse's.

38
00:02:26.402 --> 00:02:28.564
"The horse was alone before!"

39
00:02:28.924 --> 00:02:29.345
I cried.

40
00:02:29.365 --> 00:02:30.766
"Quite so.

41
00:02:31.586 --> 00:02:32.247
But what is this?"

42
00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:33.828
Holmes pointed at the ground.

43
00:02:34.609 --> 00:02:38.572
The tracks of man and horse turned sharply and headed back toward Colonel Ross' stable.

44
00:02:39.413 --> 00:02:41.915
As we followed the tracks, Holmes kept his eyes on the trail.

45
00:02:42.815 --> 00:02:44.517
I happened to look a little to one side.

46
00:02:44.537 --> 00:02:46.018
"Look, Holmes!"

47
00:02:46.418 --> 00:02:46.859
I cried.

48
00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:49.521
"They turned around again and headed toward Mapleton."

49
00:02:49.541 --> 00:02:51.262
"Very good, Watson!

50
00:02:52.063 --> 00:02:53.164
Let's follow those tracks."

51
00:02:53.996 --> 00:02:57.119
We did not have far to go because the tracks soon led us to Mapleton stable.

52
00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:02.263
As we approached, a fierce-looking elderly man strode through the gate with a riding crop in his hand.

53
00:03:02.283 --> 00:03:05.666
"We don't want any loiterers around here."

54
00:03:06.506 --> 00:03:08.468
The old man swung the riding crop menacingly.

55
00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.770
"Would you be Silas Brown, the manager?"

56
00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:12.551
asked Holmes in his sweetest voice.

57
00:03:12.571 --> 00:03:15.013
"I only need ten minutes of your time."

58
00:03:15.034 --> 00:03:17.155
"I've no time for strangers.

59
00:03:17.996 --> 00:03:19.377
Be off or I'll set the dog on you!"

60
00:03:20.198 --> 00:03:22.540
Holmes leaned forward and whispered something in the trainer's ear.

61
00:03:23.392 --> 00:03:25.593
Brown started violently and his face flushed.

62
00:03:25.613 --> 00:03:27.093
"It's a lie!"

63
00:03:27.433 --> 00:03:28.093
shouted the trainer.

64
00:03:28.113 --> 00:03:29.874
"A complete lie!"

65
00:03:29.894 --> 00:03:33.915
"Shall we argue about it here in public or talk it over in your parlor?"

66
00:03:34.255 --> 00:03:34.815
asked Holmes.

67
00:03:34.835 --> 00:03:36.816
"Oh, come in if you must."

68
00:03:36.836 --> 00:03:40.437
"I'm sure you don't mind waiting out here for me, Watson," said Holmes.

69
00:03:41.317 --> 00:03:43.797
It was twenty minutes before Holmes and the trainer reappeared.

70
00:03:45.378 --> 00:03:47.358
Such a change had come over Silas Brown!

71
00:03:48.179 --> 00:03:53.100
His face was pale, beads of perspiration shone on his brow, and his hands shook with nervousness.

72
00:03:54.048 --> 00:03:56.870
"Your instructions will be carried out," said Brown to the detective.

73
00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:01.894
"Make sure you carry them out exactly," said Holmes with a menacing tone in his voice.

74
00:04:02.794 --> 00:04:05.476
Holmes and I began to trudge back toward Colonel Ross' stable.

75
00:04:05.496 --> 00:04:11.860
"I've never met a more perfect combination of bully, coward, and sneak than Silas Brown," said Holmes.

76
00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.702
"So he has the horse?"

77
00:04:13.722 --> 00:04:15.143
"Oh yes.

78
00:04:16.043 --> 00:04:20.666
He tried to deny it at first, but there was no denying that his square-toed boots perfectly matched

79
00:04:20.706 --> 00:04:22.668
the impressions in the mud," said Holmes.

80
00:04:23.616 --> 00:04:27.019
"I described to him how he must have seen the horse on the moor early Tuesday morning.

81
00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.542
His first instinct was to return Silver Blaze to Colonel Ross.

82
00:04:32.103 --> 00:04:35.986
But then he realized that if he hid the horse until the Wessex Cup race was over, his

83
00:04:36.106 --> 00:04:37.067
own horse might win."

84
00:04:37.087 --> 00:04:40.210
"Didn't Gregory say that the police searched Brown's stable?"

85
00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:40.990
I asked.

86
00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:44.473
"Yes, but a crafty old schemer like Brown has lots of tricks."

87
00:04:44.493 --> 00:04:47.356
"Aren't you afraid that Brown will hurt Silver Blaze?"

88
00:04:48.176 --> 00:04:50.778
"He knows that his only chance of mercy is to keep the horse safe."

89
00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:52.360
I shook my head.

90
00:04:53.076 --> 00:04:56.398
"Colonel Ross doesn't seem like the kind of person who would show mercy in a case like this."

91
00:04:56.418 --> 00:05:00.241
"It's not Colonel Ross that Brown has to worry about," said Holmes.

92
00:05:00.261 --> 00:05:02.703
"I'm the person who will determine his fate.

93
00:05:03.524 --> 00:05:05.985
That is the advantage of not being an official police detective.

94
00:05:06.746 --> 00:05:10.408
I don't know if you've noticed, Watson, but the colonel has been just a bit disrespectful toward me.

95
00:05:11.949 --> 00:05:13.671
I'm going to have a little fun at his expense."

96
00:05:14.731 --> 00:05:18.314
Now that we'd found Silver Blaze, I was sure we would concentrate on finding the murderer.

97
00:05:19.115 --> 00:05:23.529
But when we arrived back at Straker's house, Holmes announced that we would return to London that night.

98
00:05:23.549 --> 00:05:26.851
"So you've given up on arresting the murderer of poor Straker?"

99
00:05:27.191 --> 00:05:28.472
said Colonel Ross with a sneer.

100
00:05:29.353 --> 00:05:30.433
Holmes shrugged his shoulders.

101
00:05:30.453 --> 00:05:34.355
"It's a difficult case, but I have every hope that your horse will run the race.

102
00:05:35.276 --> 00:05:36.697
Do you have a photograph of Straker?"

103
00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:39.498
Gregory handed him a photograph from an envelope.

104
00:05:39.518 --> 00:05:44.341
"I want to ask the maid another question before we leave for the train station," said Holmes.

105
00:05:44.361 --> 00:05:49.544
"I must say that I'm disappointed in our London detective," said Colonel Ross when Holmes was out of

106
00:05:49.624 --> 00:05:54.121
earshot. "We're not any further along on this case than before he came."

107
00:05:55.022 --> 00:05:57.883
I was about to say something in Holmes' defense when he reappeared.

108
00:05:58.803 --> 00:06:01.504
As we stepped into the carriage, a groom held the door open for us.

109
00:06:02.465 --> 00:06:04.666
"I see you keep sheep," Holmes said to the boy.

110
00:06:04.686 --> 00:06:07.147
"Have you noticed anything wrong with them lately?"

111
00:06:07.167 --> 00:06:09.648
"Only that three of them have gone lame, sir."

112
00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:14.670
"Gregory, I suggest you pay attention to that," said Holmes as the boy closed the coach door.

113
00:06:15.511 --> 00:06:16.451
Gregory looked puzzled.

114
00:06:16.471 --> 00:06:18.612
"Is there anything else I should notice?"

115
00:06:19.468 --> 00:06:22.993
"The curious incident of the dog in the nighttime," said Holmes.

116
00:06:23.013 --> 00:06:25.336
"The dog did nothing in the nighttime."

117
00:06:25.357 --> 00:06:29.222
"That was the curious incident," remarked the great detective.
